a\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda\Lambda$ ) and can be translated to a round-bottom flask equipped with a reflux condenser to enable the gram-scale synthesis of high-quality $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}$ (dobdc) (Langmuir surface area $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathbf{\Phi}}&{{}=~1947~\mathbf{\hat{m}}^{2}/\mathbf{\hat{g}},}\end{array}$ ).57 Unfortunately, we found that this approach tends to produce unidentified phases on larger $(\sim5\mathrm{~g})$ scales, likely due to poor mixing of the liters of solvent required. Extensive soaking in methanol is also needed to completely remove strongly bound DMF from the pores. As such, we aimed to identify a reliable and scalable synthesis of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ that employs smaller volumes of organic solvents with the ultimate goal of bypassing the use of DMF entirely.
We first turned to mechanochemistry, as several mechanochemical syntheses of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ and related MOFs have been reported.58 As outlined in Section 4, we found that amines can serve as both the base required to deprotonate $\mathrm{H}_{4}$ dobdc and the liquid needed for LAG, enabling the rapid synthesis of porous $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ and related MOFs via mechanical grinding in a planetary ball mill.42,39 High surface area $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{do}\bar{\mathrm{b}}\mathrm{dc}\big)$ (Langmuir surface area $=1992\mathrm{\m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ ) can be produced on a gram scale in just 5 min of grinding time using this approach. The main limitation of this strategy is the need for specialized equipment: further scaling up of this reaction would have necessitated a ball mill with larger milling jars. As such, we aimed to improve the scalability of the solvothermal synthesis of $\mathrm{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{d}\bar{\mathrm{ob}}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}\big)$ , which does not require any equipment beyond a flask and a stir bar.
As discussed in Section 3, the attempted synthesis of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ at ${\bf>}0.1{\bf M}$ linker concentrations leads instead to a new framework, CORN-MOF-1 $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}\right)$ , that can subsequently be converted to $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ by heating in DMF.32 This twostep route reproducibly enables the synthesis of high-quality $\mathrm{{Mg}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ (Langmuir surface area $\dot{=}2060\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g},$ on ${\sim}s_{\mathrm{~g~}}$ scale and thus became our preferred approach to prepare this MOF. However, this synthetic route does suffer from two major drawbacks: (1) the use of DMF, which necessitates extensive soaking in methanol, and (2) long reaction times, as at least 5 days of heating in DMF are needed to fully convert CORN-MOF-1 $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}\right)$ to $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ . In our hands, the volume of methanol required for soaking could not be minimized using Soxhlet extraction due to the degradation of the MOF under these conditions.
Building upon these high-concentration results (Section 3) and our mechanochemical studies (Section 4), we hypothesized that the addition of exogenous base at high reaction concentrations should help to bypass the formation of CORNMOF-1 $\left(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}\right)$ , which has partially deprotonated linkers. Indeed, we found that the addition of sodium hydroxide enables the synthesis of $\mathrm{{Mg}}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})$ under high-concentration $(1.0\mathrm{M})$ conditions in water at room temperature, eliminating the use of DMF entirely. This route was employed to produce $>100\mathrm{~g~}$ of $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{g}_{2}(\mathbf{dobdc})$ in a single batch (Langmuir surface area $=2379\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ in just $24\mathrm{~h~}$ of synthesis time.54 Based on this success, we expect that many other MOFs can be prepared under high-concentration conditions, potentially with the addition of a stoichiometric base.
## 7. CONCLUSION
As outlined above, many user-friendly avenues to the scalable synthesis of MOFs, including high-concentration solvothermal (Section 3), mechanochemical (Section 4), and ionothermal (Section 5) syntheses, have emerged in recent years. Each of these approaches possesses its own advantages and potential drawbacks. If a researcher is starting with a known solvothermal synthesis and aiming to increase the amount of MOF that can be prepared in a single batch, the easiest route to try first is high-concentration solvothermal synthesis (Section 3). In this manner, no other reaction parameters besides the amounts of reagents employed need to be changed to potentially produce MOFs on a gram scale. However, as noted in our discussion about the attempted high-concentration synthesis of $\mathbf{Mg}_{2}\big(\mathrm{dobdc}\big)$ , this approach may lead to alternative phases if the desired phase is not thermodynamically favored enough at high reaction concentrations. Mechanochemical synthesis offers the advantages of fast reaction times and minimal solvent use but tends to produce poorly crystalline MOFs and requires specialized equipment such as a ball mill or twin-screw extruder, which may be constrictive for researchers without ready access to these instruments. On the other hand, ionothermal MOF synthesis does not require any specialized equipment, but it does necessitate access to low-melting metal salt precursors to enable MOF formation in the molten metal salt medium. Overall, access to multiple avenues to easily produce MOFs on a gram scale will guarantee that at least one route will work for a given framework.
Moving forward, more in-depth studies into the chemical pathways that guide self-assembly, such as the role of acid modulators (Section 2), are needed to streamline access to MOFs with ever-improving properties. We encourage all researchers in the field to consider the user-friendliness and potential scalability of all reported MOF syntheses to enable others, especially nonexperts, to prepare and study MOFs for next-generation applications.
